        },
        {
            "number": 3,
            "revision": "7957751d571ee0df314c92651214ac731dacb296",
            "parents": [
                "20d2e853c25fbc0ae4d566f99a4b0834c9e21676"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/15/90515/3",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Angela Cheng wiwynn",
                "email": "angelaptcheng@gmail.com",
                "username": "AngelaPTCheng"
            },
            "createdOn": 1779870064,
            "author": {
                "name": "Angela Cheng wiwynn",
                "email": "angelaptcheng@gmail.com",
                "username": "AngelaPTCheng"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "common/src/software_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 157,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "I am not understanding the need of this change here. This is a single threaded code and i don't see any ctx.spawn in this path anywhere before below which already handles and avoids duplicate device initialization.\n\nif (devices.contains(config.objectPath))\n    {\n        error(\"Device configured from {PATH} is already known\", \"PATH\",\n              config.objectPath);\n        co_return;\n    }\n    \n    \nSo, how can two input sources jump over each other's foot here?\n\n@angelaptcheng@gmail.com Did you notice any issue because of which you are making this change?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "common/src/software_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 157,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Angela Cheng wiwynn",
                        "email": "angelaptcheng@gmail.com",
                        "username": "AngelaPTCheng"
                    },
                    "message": "The race occurs when a configuration object appears after interfaceAddedMatch is spawned but is also returned by get_sub_tree(). In this window, both paths call handleInterfaceAdded() for the same path, and since the first call hasn't completed initDevice() yet, the second call also passes the devices.contains() check and enters initDevice()."
                },
                {
                    "file": "common/src/software_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 157,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "> The race occurs when a configuration object appears after interfaceAddedMatch is spawned but is also returned by get_sub_tree(). In this window, both paths call handleInterfaceAdded() for the same path, and since the first call hasn't completed initDevice() yet, the second call also passes the devices.contains() check and enters initDevice().\n\n\nThanks for clarifying. How about using a async mutex https://grok.openbmc.org/xref/openbmc/sdbusplus/test/async/mutex.cpp?r=540ad059e6e423141e3f31f906a81961b552a749 to guard the section starting at line 184 (before the check for \"if (devices.contains(config.objectPath)\") ?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "common/src/software_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 157,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Angela Cheng wiwynn",
                        "email": "angelaptcheng@gmail.com",
                        "username": "AngelaPTCheng"
                    },
                    "message": "Thanks for the suggestion.\n\nI tried the async mutex approach, and it does prevent the duplicate initDevice() issue. However, I observed intermittent delays because a single mutex serializes all device initializations within the same process, not only duplicate initialization for the same path.\n\nFor example, in the i2cvr updater, some devices had to wait for the previous initDevice() to finish before starting:\ninitDevice done for ...Yosemite5_MB_VR_VDD18_S5, took 1014ms\nlock acquired for ...Yosemite5_CXL_VR_P1V2_VDD_DDR_CH02, waited 1014ms\n\ninitDevice done for ...Yosemite5_CXL_VR_P1V2_VDD_DDR_CH02, took 1346ms\nlock acquired for ...Yosemite5_Paddle_HSC_P12V_AUX, waited 1009ms\n\ninitDevice done for ...Yosemite5_Paddle_HSC_P12V_AUX, took 2290ms\nlock acquired for ...Yosemite5_CXL_VR_P1V2_VDD_DDR_CH13, waited 982ms\n\nThe previous initializingPaths approach only suppresses concurrent initialization for the same path, which is the race condition we are trying to avoid, while still allowing different paths to initialize concurrently.\n\nDo you think the serialization overhead from the single mutex is acceptable here, or would a per-path guard such as initializingPaths be more appropriate?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "common/src/software_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 157,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Angela Cheng wiwynn",
                        "email": "angelaptcheng@gmail.com",
                        "username": "AngelaPTCheng"
                    },
                    "message": "@paligill@gmail.com, could you please take another look at the async mutex test results above?\n\nThe mutex approach fixes the duplicate initDevice() issue, but it also serializes initialization for different paths in the same process and caused intermittent delays in the i2cvr updater.\n\nDo you think this overhead is acceptable, or would you prefer a per-path guard such as initializingPaths?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "common/src/software_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 157,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "@angelaptcheng@gmail.com How about if we do this way https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/phosphor-bmc-code-mgmt/+/90892, i understand we need to refactor some code around but we can avoid using another data structure as that can create confusion and future bugs. Also it will make it safe on remove interface side as well."
                },
                {
                    "file": "common/src/software_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 157,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Angela Cheng wiwynn",
                        "email": "angelaptcheng@gmail.com",
                        "username": "AngelaPTCheng"
                    },
                    "message": "Thanks for the suggestion.\n\nThis approach looks good to us. We will use it and test the duplicate initDevice() race case on our machine."
                },
                {
                    "file": "common/src/software_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 270,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "Do you see any loophole in remove interface path too?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "common/src/software_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 270,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Angela Cheng wiwynn",
                        "email": "angelaptcheng@gmail.com",
                        "username": "AngelaPTCheng"
                    },
                    "message": "I have not observed any issue from the remove interface path in the logs.\n\nFrom the current code, handleInterfaceRemoved() itself does not have any co_await, so it should not be suspended in the middle of its own removal sequence.\n\nThe only theoretical interaction I can see is between handleInterfaceAdded() and handleInterfaceRemoved(). If InterfacesRemoved is handled while handleInterfaceAdded() is suspended, for example at co_await createInventoryAssociations(), the remove path could erase the device entry while the add/init flow still expects it to exist.\n\nIn practice, I think this is unlikely during normal boot initialization. In our case, the duplicate initDevice() issue happens when the same configuration object is handled from both the ObjectMapper scan and the InterfacesAdded match. The remove-side interaction would require the same configuration object to be removed while initialization is still in progress, which I have not observed during the normal boot flow."
                },
                {
                    "file": "common/src/software_manager.cpp",
                    "line": 270,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Jagpal S Gill",
                        "email": "paligill@gmail.com",
                        "username": "jagpalgill"
                    },
                    "message": "Acknowledged"
                }
            ],
